description:
Background: Our TypeScript SDK needs a robust, ergonomic way to let users consume server streaming responses as either async iterators (for idiomatic TS code) or as Web Streams (for browser/cross-runtime code) without surprising differences in cancellation, backpressure, or multi-consumer semantics. Existing community patterns either duplicate buffering logic or make incorrect assumptions about ownership when multiple consumers subscribe.

Goal: Design and implement a lightweight shim (CheckpointedStream) that provides: single-producer semantics, multi-consumer read checkpoints, consistent cancellation propagation, backpressure-aware reads, and an ergonomic API to convert between ReadableStream <-> AsyncIterator with explicit ownership-transfer semantics. Must work in Node (ReadableStream/AsyncIterator), Deno, and browsers.

Non-goals: Full-featured stream multiplexer for arbitrary rewinding or persistent storage; durable checkpoints beyond in-memory. Not implementing server-side replay features in this ticket.

Context: There are many existing internal tickets about bridging async iterables and Web Streams (see dependencies). This ticket focuses on the multi-consumer case where consumers may be long-running but only need deterministic semantics (either clone/clone-with-checkpoint or take-ownership).

Example ergonomics (developer-facing):

1) As AsyncIterator with single consumer (simple):

const resp = await client.streamGenerate(prompt);
for await (const chunk of resp.stream) {
  // chunk.text, chunk.delta, chunk.done
}

2) Convert to WebStream in browser:

const webStream = resp.stream.toWebStream();
const reader = webStream.getReader();
while (true) {
  const { value, done } = await reader.read();
  if (done) break;
  // handle chunk (value)
}

3) Two consumer pattern with checkpoints:

// primary consumer reads live and applies diffs
const c = resp.stream.createCheckpoint();
for await (const delta of resp.stream) applyDelta(delta);

// secondary consumer starts later from checkpoint without re-running the request
const from = resp.stream.checkpoint(c);
for await (const item of from) renderTranscript(item);

Design intent: checkponts are lightweight cursors into the producer sequence; they do not buffer the entire stream by default. When a checkpoint prevents freeing of earlier chunks, the shim will keep a small retention window and apply eviction heuristics.
